---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Code from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Code.astro";
import Note from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Note.astro";
import Figure from "@dario-loi/polytope/astro/Figure.astro";
import { u } from "../../data/nav";
---
<Docs title="Python and figures" lede="Paper figures, notebooks, and Python demos use the same colors and type as the web pages.">
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <Code lang="sh" code={`pip install "polytope-theme[matplotlib,plotly]"`} />
  <p>The package has no required dependencies. The extras are <code>matplotlib</code>, <code>plotly</code>, <code>gradio</code>, <code>streamlit</code>, and <code>all</code>.</p>

  <h2>matplotlib</h2>
  <Code lang="python" code={`import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import polytope_theme as ply
from polytope_theme.mpl import label_lines

ply.use("light")            # or "dark" for slides and dark pages

fig, ax = plt.subplots()
for name in ["GCN", "GAT", "SCN"]:
    ax.plot(x, results[name], label=name)
label_lines(ax)            # direct labels at the line ends, in text color
fig.savefig("figure.pdf")`} />
  <p>The style sets the categorical color cycle, a quiet y grid, no top or right spines, and Libre Franklin when it is installed. Install the font from Google Fonts for exact type; matplotlib falls back to DejaVu Sans otherwise.</p>
  <Figure num={1}>
    <picture>
      <source srcset={u("/img/example-dark.png")} media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
      <img src={u("/img/example-light.png")} alt="Synthetic example: a line chart with three labeled series and a bar chart, drawn with the light matplotlib style." width="1200" height="500" loading="lazy" />
    </picture>
    <Fragment slot="caption">The light style on synthetic data. Lines carry direct labels; bars use the first categorical color.</Fragment>
  </Figure>

  <h3>Colormaps</h3>
  <Code lang="python" code={`ax.imshow(adjacency, cmap="ply-sequential")   # magnitude: one hue, light to dark
ax.imshow(signed, cmap="ply-diverging")       # sign: cobalt to tomato, gray at zero`} />

  <h2>plotly</h2>
  <Code lang="python" code={`import plotly.express as px
from polytope_theme import plotly_theme

plotly_theme.register()                     # adds "polytope" and "polytope_dark"
px.line(df, x="epoch", y="loss", color="model", template="polytope")`} />

  <h2>Gradio</h2>
  <Code lang="python" code={`import gradio as gr
from polytope_theme import gradio_theme

with gr.Blocks() as demo:
    ...
demo.launch(theme=gradio_theme.theme("auto"))   # gradio 4 and 5: gr.Blocks(theme=...)`} />

  <h2>Streamlit</h2>
  <Code lang="python" code={`from polytope_theme import streamlit_theme
streamlit_theme.write_config(".streamlit/config.toml", mode="light")
# refuses to replace a config.toml it did not write; pass overwrite=True to force`} />

  <h2>Rules that carry over</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Series colors go in the fixed order. Past eight series, <code>ply.categorical()</code> raises an error: fold the rest into "Other" or split into small multiples.</li>
    <li>Scatter plots and small multiples use the first three colors only. Any two points can sit side by side there, and only three colors stay distinct for every reader.</li>
    <li>Status colors (good, warning, serious, critical) mean state, never "series 4", and always come with a label or marker.</li>
  </ul>
  <Note kind="soft">The <a href={u("/foundations/charts/")}>Charts</a> foundation explains how the palette was checked for color vision deficiency.</Note>
</Docs>
